# short-description: EFI disk image for genericx86-64 with a persistent /data
# long-description: meta-yocto-bsp's genericx86.wks.in without a /data
# partition, which overlayfs-etc requires. The layout is Moonforge's: ESP,
# rootfs, then /data as the third partition; it is mounted by its "data"
# label (OVERLAYFS_ETC_DEVICE in kas/common/tessaro.yml). The same image
# boots on hardware and under QEMU (kas/machine/genericx86-64.yml).
#
# /data is also the last partition. There is no swap partition after it, so
# `tessaro-ctl storage grow` can extend it over whatever the disk has past
# the image.
#
# GPT plus an ESP, so this boots on UEFI firmware only. genericx86-64 declares
# the pcbios MACHINE_FEATURE, but no legacy boot sector is installed here.
#
# --part-type is the EFI System Partition type GUID. wic only sets a GPT type
# code when a wks asks for one, so without it the boot partition is typed
# "Microsoft basic data" - which OVMF happily boots from anyway, but firmware
# that follows the spec and looks for an ESP by type GUID will not. Upstream's
# genericx86.wks.in and Moonforge's qemu one both omit it; this image runs on
# hardware, so it is set here. The fs label is "boot" rather than upstream's
# confusing "msdos".
#
# Nothing here names a disk at runtime, which is the point on hardware that may
# boot off SATA, USB or NVMe: --use-uuid on / and /boot makes wic write
# root=PARTUUID=... into grub.cfg and UUID=... into /etc/fstab, and
# --use-label on /data makes the generated fstab say LABEL=data, the label
# the preinit looks up on root's disk (OVERLAYFS_ETC_DEVICE). Drop
# --use-label and the fstab entry
# reverts to /dev/sda3, which systemd then fails to mount on an NVMe board even
# though the preinit already mounted /data by label.
#
# In-place updates (tessaro-ctl update) also need:
#
# * Every identifier the root filesystem names is fixed: the PARTUUIDs with
#   --uuid (grub.cfg's root=), and the /boot vfat serial with --fsuuid (the
#   UUID= line in fstab). Without them wic makes new ones
#   on every build, and an update replaces the root filesystem - fstab
#   included - but never the partition table or the ESP's filesystem, so a
#   new rootfs would name another build's /boot and fail to mount it. They are
#   distinct per machine, which also makes the updater's layout check a
#   machine check.
# * The root partition is TESSARO_ROOTFS_SIZE, not whatever this build's
#   rootfs happened to need: a later image has to fit into the partition that
#   is already on the disk. The ESP is TESSARO_ESP_SIZE, with room for a
#   second kernel, which the updater writes beside the old one before the
#   rename.
#
# Changing any of these is a new disk layout: every device needs one full
# reflash before it can take updates again, and the updater says so.

part /boot --source bootimg-efi --sourceparams="loader=${EFI_PROVIDER}" --ondisk sda --label boot --part-type=C12A7328-F81F-11D2-BA4B-00A0C93EC93B --active --align 1024 --use-uuid --uuid b3d3f14e-50a1-4e40-8f3a-923193673604 --fsuuid 0x37CBDAD6 --fixed-size ${TESSARO_ESP_SIZE}
part / --source rootfs --ondisk sda --fstype=ext4 --label platform --align 1024 --use-uuid --uuid 1522c8e6-a2fd-49a9-a9c5-4f3a5e7c5eaf --fixed-size ${TESSARO_ROOTFS_SIZE}
part /data --ondisk sda --fstype=ext4 --size "${IMAGE_DATA_MIN_SIZE}" --label data --use-label --align 1024 --uuid 5ae264f5-44ea-4f3b-a2d9-f8817aaebd8f

bootloader --ptable gpt --timeout=5 --append="rootwait rootfstype=ext4 console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0"
